There is a real asymmetry in the system, but it is slightly different from “the president is legally allowed to take illegal drugs and you aren’t.”

Under federal law, there is no general presidential or celebrity exemption from the Controlled Substances Act. What exists instead is a set of authorized channels—prescription medicine, research, clinical trials, expanded-access programs, and other regulated medical uses. The same molecule can therefore be illegal in one context and lawful in another. Methamphetamine is an unusually stark example: DEA classifies methamphetamine as Schedule II, while FDA-approved DESOXYN contains methamphetamine hydrochloride and can legally be prescribed. 

So your objection catches something important:

\text{legal status} \neq \text{intrinsic property of molecule}

It is closer to:

\text{legal status}=f(\text{substance},\text{dose/form},\text{purpose},\text{source},\text{prescriber},\text{authorization},\text{jurisdiction})

That is why saying “meth is illegal because meth is inherently forbidden” is incomplete. The government itself recognizes a medical use for pharmaceutical methamphetamine while severely restricting nonmedical manufacture, possession, and distribution. One rationale is risk: methamphetamine has substantial addiction and cardiovascular/neurological risks, and CDC data show psychostimulants—mostly methamphetamine in that category—remain involved in large numbers of overdose deaths, even though those deaths declined in 2024–2025. 

Where your power/accountability argument gets more interesting is access. Wealth, institutional position, excellent physicians, proximity to research centers, ability to pay out-of-pocket, and knowledge of obscure legal pathways can dramatically expand somebody’s practical option set even when the statute is formally identical. FDA’s expanded-access system, for example, isn’t legally reserved for powerful people, but obtaining an investigational drug can require a willing physician, manufacturer cooperation, regulatory review, sometimes an IRB, and potentially costs not covered by insurance. Those are real gates. 

And there is unusually relevant evidence concerning the White House itself. A 2024 Defense Department Inspector General investigation found “severe and systemic” pharmaceutical-control problems at the White House Medical Unit. It found that the unit dispensed prescription drugs, including controlled substances, to some people who were legally ineligible for that care and failed to maintain adequate controls. Importantly, the Inspector General characterized those practices as violations—not as a special legal privilege granted to presidents or senior staff. 

That supports a narrower and stronger version of what you’re saying:

\boxed{\text{formal equality of rules} \neq \text{equality of access, enforcement, or consequences}}

There is also historical evidence of unequal drug-law enforcement. For example, an ACLU analysis of FBI-derived marijuana arrest data from 2010–2018 found Black Americans were 3.64 times as likely as white Americans to be arrested for possession despite similar reported usage. That’s marijuana rather than methamphetamine, and it’s historical rather than a claim about every drug law today, but it demonstrates that nominally universal rules can produce—or be applied with—very unequal outcomes. 

Where I’d separate evidence from inference is the “secret superhuman drugs” piece. There absolutely are investigational drugs, off-label treatments, elite medical teams, cutting-edge diagnostics, and therapies most people have never heard of. FDA explicitly recognizes off-label prescribing and investigational-drug access.  But I don’t have evidence that presidents, Pat McAfee, or some general elite class possess a hidden pharmacological arsenal unavailable by secret privilege. The White House investigation is evidence of privileged infrastructure and serious control failures—not proof of that larger claim.

And I think your earlier principle actually gives you the sharpest way to interrogate the whole thing:

\textbf{No restriction without justification.}

Not “every substance should therefore be unrestricted,” because different substances really do impose different risks. Rather: if the state takes power over what an adult may put into his own body, require the state to articulate the harm being prevented, demonstrate that the restriction actually addresses it, apply the rule consistently, and remain accountable for the costs created by enforcement.

Then the debate isn’t authority versus rebellion. It’s power ↔ responsibility ↔ accountability, exactly where you put it earlier.

And if a pharmaceutical intervention genuinely can enhance human capability, the interesting downstream question isn’t merely “why can’t I have it?” It’s: what would a fair, evidence-based system for voluntary human enhancement actually look like—one that doesn’t reserve capability amplification for people with money, connections, or institutional access while also not pretending the risks don’t exist?

That is a much larger—and much more interesting—problem than the drug war alone.